        "title": "The Light of Karbala and Spiritual Journey - 9th Muharram",
        "date": "25-06-2026",
        "image": "https://res.cloudinary.com/dlafcjt6z/image/upload/v1783929558/columns_fpkl28.png",
        "excerpt": "...",
        "content": `
        <div style="font-family: 'Arial', sans-serif; font-size: 1.1em; line-height: 1.8; text-align: left; direction: ltr;">    
            <p><strong>The Light of Karbala and Spiritual Journey - 9th Muharram</strong></p>
            <p><strong>Written by: Haji Shabbir Ahmed Shigree</strong></p>
            
            <p>The nights of sorrow in the land of Nineveh are reaching their peak, and today's tragic episode of the 9th of Muharram is dedicated to the sufferings and remembrance of the ocean of loyalty, the commander of the Hussaini army, the water-bearer of Sakina (AS), Bab al-Hawaiej Hazrat Abbas Alamdar (AS). There is a special and heart-touching way to present oneself on the holy land of Karbala. The pilgrims first present themselves at the court of Ghazi Abbas Alamdar (AS) and offer salutations, and after attaining the honor of his visitation (Ziyarat), they set off for the visitation of their Master, Imam Hussain (AS). When a person exits the shrine of Abbas (AS) and moves through Bayn al-Haramayn towards the holy shrine of the oppressed Imam (AS), the pilgrim is commanded to take very small steps instead of walking fast; because for every single step you take on this pure soil, the Lord of the Universe grants the reward of an accepted Hajj in the pilgrim's record of deeds. Glory be to Allah! Such is the status of the pilgrims of Hussain (AS).</p>
            
            <p>Hazrat Abbas (AS) is that great and exalted personality upon whose unparalleled loyalty and immortal bravery the Family of Muhammad (AS) themselves take pride. History is a witness that if there apparently seems to be any difference between Master Hasan (AS), Master Hussain (AS), and Master Abbas (AS), it is solely of "Imamate"; otherwise, Ghazi Abbas (AS) was also the pure blood of the Tongue of God, the Victorious Lion of God, Master Ali Karrar (AS). The Holy God has bestowed such a high metaphysical and spiritual status upon the standard-bearer of Karbala that he is called "Bab al-Hawaiej" (The Door for Fulfilling Needs) in the universe. Even today, when aggrieved hearts from all over the world hold the hem of his holy shrine and offer his intercession, no beggar returns empty-handed from the Divine Court, and all prayers are fulfilled.</p>
            
            <p>In the desert of Nineveh, when the time of sacrifice was completed and all the helpers, companions, and youths of Imam Hussain (AS) were martyred in the way of God one by one, the embodiment of loyalty, Hazrat Abbas (AS), came to his oppressed brother and weepingly asked for permission to fight. The exalted Imam (AS) looked at his brave brother, tears welled up in his eyes, and he said: "Abbas! You are the commander and standard-bearer of my army; if you go, the solace of the innocent children in the tents will break, their courage will be shattered." But when Ghazi Abbas (AS) insisted, giving the sake of the children's thirst and his own helplessness, the Imam (AS) said with a heavy heart: "Abbas! If you really must go now, then not for fighting, but arrange water from somewhere for my innocent Sakina (AS)." Hazrat Abbas (AS) came to the tents for the final farewell. As soon as he came before sister Zainab (AS), the reflection of Zahra (SA) agonizingly kissed both arms of her brave, lion-hearted brother and began to cry bitterly.</p>
            
            <p>Lady Zainab (AS) remembered that tragic night of the 21st of Ramadan when dear father Master Ali (AS), in a wounded state on the bed of martyrdom in Kufa, made his final will, saying: "Zainab! Your brothers will be killed, the tents will burn, and your veil will be snatched..." Lady Zainab (AS) held the shoulders of Ghazi and said: "Abbas! Hearing those words of father, I used to think that a sister who has a brave brother like Abbas (AS) present, who in the world has the audacity to even imagine snatching Zainab's (AS) veil! But Abbas (AS), today you are also leaving..." Ghazi Abbas (AS), with the majesty of Haider, galloped his horse and reached the banks of Alqama. He pushed back the enemy's army, filled the waterskin, and when he returned towards the tents, thousands of enemies surrounded him from all sides on the orders of Umar ibn Sa'ad. His (AS) attention was solely on saving the waterskin when suddenly an accursed man sneakily attacked from behind a tree, severing his right arm. He (AS) took the standard in his left hand, and with the second strike, his left arm was also martyred. But Ghazi (AS) did not lose courage; he clamped the strap of the waterskin in his teeth so that the water could reach the children.</p>
            
            <p>Meanwhile, in the camp, when the four-year-old innocent Lady Sakina (AS) saw the standard of her Uncle Ghazi dipping and fluttering on the distant horizon, the innocent's heart trembled; she cried out in agony and, turning towards the battlefield, called out: "Dear Uncle! For God's sake come back, I don't want water anymore, I will never ask you for water again, just let my uncle come back..." When the Yazidis saw that Abbas (AS) had no arms left, they unleashed a rain of arrows. One cruel arrow hit the forehead of Ghazi Abbas (AS), a poisoned arrow pierced his pure eye, and another arrow tore through the waterskin and struck his blessed chest. Having no arms, Master Abbas (AS) could not pull those arrows from his eye and chest. In this state of helplessness, a wretched accursed man stepped forward and struck the heavy iron mace on the illuminated head of Ghazi (AS), cleaving the blessed head. Due to having no arms, when Ghazi (AS) fell from the horse's saddle onto the burning sand, he called out to his Master in agony: "Master! Accept my last salutation..." When Imam Hussain (AS) arrived, lifting his brother's body was not possible; the body of Abbas (AS) remained there on the bank of the river, and Hussain (AS) returned to the tents alone with his hand on his back... Under the shadow of this painful memory of the 9th of Muharram and the standard-bearing of Ghazi Abbas (AS), special prayers were offered for all attendees, pilgrims, and believers. To be continued...</p>
        </div>
        `
